WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.432
A synchronous request returns after the promised
operation finishes; an asynchronous request can

00:00:06.432 --> 00:00:10.519
return once the system accepts responsibility for
later work.

00:00:10.519 --> 00:00:11.725
Both can use HTTP.

00:00:11.725 --> 00:00:16.616
The important distinction is the contract, not
whether a response exists.

00:00:16.616 --> 00:00:22.781
Sending an email, preparing a large download or
provisioning a virtual machine can return an

00:00:22.781 --> 00:00:27.203
operation identifier while the requested work
continues elsewhere.

00:00:27.203 --> 00:00:32.525
A queue lets a producer describe work and hand it to
consumers that run later.

00:00:32.525 --> 00:00:39.008
The message schema is an interface contract,
including identifiers, required fields and version

00:00:39.008 --> 00:00:39.622
behavior.

00:00:39.622 --> 00:00:46.241
In the tweet example, one service accepts
publication work, another expands it into feed
updates,

00:00:46.241 --> 00:00:49.175
and another handles optional notifications.

00:00:49.175 --> 00:00:54.361
This separates stages with different cost, latency
and failure requirements.
